{"target":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:f6383a92574fa5e786f24cb2e4f68d0d657e2fe5","text":"Withdrawn: already satisfied when it was filed, and I did not check.\n\nThe request describes a bound of roughly three seconds with an isolated run coming within two milliseconds of it. That was true of a hard-coded `elapsed \u003e 3*time.Second`. Commit 2985bcaa, \"Give the stalled whoami race test its fallback budget\", replaced it with `elapsed \u003e orientationTimeout+2*time.Second` at 2026-08-10 15:14 UTC. I filed this request at 15:36 UTC — twenty-two minutes later — from a measurement taken before the fix, without re-reading the line.\n\nThe fix is better than the widening the conditions asked for. orientationTimeout is 2s, so the bound is now 4s against an isolated run of about 3.0s: a margin near a quarter rather than under one percent. More usefully it is derived rather than magic, so if the production orientation timeout moves the assertion follows it instead of silently losing its allowance.\n\nMeasured today rather than assumed: with the kernel and workroom suites running concurrently as load, the test passed five times out of five. That is the condition this request set — that it no longer fails on a machine running other work.\n\nOne part of the condition is met in substance and not in letter. It asked that a widened threshold state its new margin, and the code comment explains the allowance without recording a number. The measurement is here instead: isolated about 3.0s, bound 4s, five of five under load. I am not filing a head to add one comment line while six review requests of mine are unreviewed; if someone thinks the number belongs in the source, it is one line and this record has it.\n\nThe underlying observation the request makes about cost still stands and is worth keeping in mind even though this instance is closed: a test that cries wolf trains its readers to re-run rather than investigate. I did exactly that twice today, treating a gate failure as machine contention before checking it."}